//~ compile_fail(backend)
//~ error[KORU030]: Resource 'reply' obligation

// NEGATIVE (must FAIL to compile): do the Tversion exchange and never give the
// request back. This is the INNERMOST of the three nestings and the one whose
// leak the C reports only as a log line, at teardown, in a build with logging
// on.
//
// `uk_9p_version` is the ONLY exported `uk_9p_*` verb that does not remove its
// own request. Compare `uk_9p_attach` at 9p.c:169 and `uk_9p_walk` at 9p.c:272,
// both of which call `uk_9pdev_req_remove` before returning. `version` hands the
// caller a live `struct uk_9preq *` and the caller owes the removal.
//
// WHAT IT COSTS TO FORGET:
//
//   * The tag stays set in `dev->_req_mgmt.tag_bm`. That is a bitmap of
//     UK_9P_NUMTAGS entries, allocated by `_req_mgmt_next_tag_locked`
//     (9pdev.c:172) with `uk_find_next_zero_bit`. It is a genuinely finite
//     resource and nothing ever reclaims a tag except `uk_9pdev_req_remove`.
//   * The request stays on `req_list`, so at disconnect `_req_mgmt_cleanup`
//     walks it, finds a live reference, and prints
//     `uk_pr_err("Tag %d still has references on cleanup.\n")` (9pdev.c:186) —
//     which is a message about a mistake made at start-up, delivered at
//     shutdown, in a build with `uk_pr_err` compiled in.
//
// There is no assertion for it.
//
// WHY THIS IS A COMPILE ERROR RATHER THAN A SILENT INSERTION, which is the
// distinction `unikraft/alloc`'s README draws and this module inherits: a
// dropped `<received!>` has exactly ONE consumer, `reply.release`, and
// `reply.release` is not a disposal — it mints `<negotiated!>`, another live
// obligation on a different handle. There is therefore nothing for
// auto-discharge to elect, and the dropped reply is REPORTED.
//
// The same shape is what makes the composition rule self-enforcing rather than
// advisory. Even if the obligation were silently discharged, this program would
// still be stuck: `version` consumed the `*Dev`, and `reply.release` is the only
// tor in the module that produces one. A leaked reply is a program with no
// device left to attach with, to skip with, or to disconnect.
//
// EXPECTED: error[KORU030]: Resource 'reply' obligation <received!> was not
//           discharged. Call: reply.release
//
// (The diagnostic names the OUTPUT FIELD of the branch arm, `reply`, not the
// arm binding `v` the flow spells. Worth knowing before writing an expectation
// against a record-carrying branch.)

// The `unikraft` namespace is declared here, not inherited: koruc's built-in
// alias probes the sibling koru-libs CHECKOUT, so in a worktree this file would
// otherwise compile against main's lifts instead of the ones beside it.
~std/compiler:paths {
    unikraft: {{ ENTRY }}/../..
}

~import unikraft/ninep
~import std/io

~unikraft/ninep:connect(tag: "korufs")
| ok mount |> unikraft/ninep:version(dev: mount, requested: "9P2000.L")
    | negotiated v |> std/io:print.ln("version: {{ v.protocol:s }}, msize {{ v.msize:d }}")
    | refused e |> std/io:print.ln("version: {{ e.reason:s }}")
        |> unikraft/ninep:session.skipped(e.dev): spent
            |> unikraft/ninep:disconnect(dev: spent)
| absent why |> std/io:print.ln("connect: {{ why:s }}")
| refused e |> std/io:print.ln("connect: {{ e.reason:s }}")
